1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,760
This is ECE 358, video 16 a. The topic is packet forwarding and longest prefix matching. It covers slides 49 to 58 of chapters 4 and 5.

2
00:00:14,210 --> 00:00:29,229
After this video, you can read a routing table. You can test if a destination address matches a table entry. And you can choose the output interface with longest prefix matching. The question bank asks all of these.

3
00:00:29,679 --> 00:00:40,345
Packet forwarding is the relaying of packets from one physical interface to another, by routers on the Internet. Here, packet P arrives at router B.

4
00:00:40,795 --> 00:00:44,806
Router B has three interfaces, numbered 1, 2 and 3.

5
00:00:45,256 --> 00:00:51,443
How does router B choose the next hop for P? The next hop is a function f of two inputs.

6
00:00:51,893 --> 00:00:55,754
The first input is the destination address in packet P.

7
00:00:56,204 --> 00:01:02,241
The second input is the routing table in the router. Here, it is the routing table of B.

8
00:01:02,691 --> 00:01:13,230
The router reads only the destination IP address from the header of P. Here, the destination is 73 dot 2 dot 5 dot 9.

9
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:26,544
The router looks up this address in its routing table. The address starts with 73 dot 2. So it matches the first row, 73 dot 2 dot 0 dot 0, slash 16.

10
00:01:26,994 --> 00:01:34,951
This row gives interface 1 and the next router I P 1. So P leaves on interface 1, to I P 1.

11
00:01:35,401 --> 00:01:44,660
This method is called table-driven routing. The routing table decides the output interface. The next slides explain the function f.

12
00:01:45,110 --> 00:01:53,217
Slide 50 also names another forwarding technique: source routing. Here, the source host finds the complete path.

13
00:01:53,667 --> 00:02:01,731
The source puts the path in the header of the packet, in the option field. Here, the path is R 1, R 2, R 3.

14
00:02:02,181 --> 00:02:06,511
The source sends the packet to the first router of the path, R 1.

15
00:02:06,961 --> 00:02:09,415
R 1 reads the path in the packet.

16
00:02:09,865 --> 00:02:14,131
R 1 sends the packet to the next router of the path, R 2.

17
00:02:14,581 --> 00:02:19,957
R 2 and R 3 do the same, until the packet arrives at the destination.

18
00:02:20,407 --> 00:02:27,554
In this course, routers use table-driven routing. Source routing is only an example of another technique.

19
00:02:28,004 --> 00:02:35,663
Slide 51 shows the simple structure of a routing table, for router B. Each row has five columns.

20
00:02:36,113 --> 00:02:40,315
The destination address and the mask give a destination network.

21
00:02:40,765 --> 00:02:43,005
The next hop is the next router.

22
00:02:43,455 --> 00:02:46,079
The interface is the output port of B.

23
00:02:46,529 --> 00:02:48,769
The metric is the cost of the route.

24
00:02:49,219 --> 00:03:01,038
Row 1: the destination is 73 dot 2 dot 0 dot 0, and the mask is 255 dot 255 dot 0 dot 0.

25
00:03:01,488 --> 00:03:15,312
Each 255 is 8 one bits. So the mask has 8 plus 8, which is 16 one bits. Row 1 is the network 73 dot 2 dot 0 dot 0, slash 16.

26
00:03:15,762 --> 00:03:19,687
The next hop is router I P 1, on interface 1.

27
00:03:20,137 --> 00:03:25,663
B learns this entry by running routing protocols. The star marks it as learned.

28
00:03:26,113 --> 00:03:38,742
Row 2 is the network 129 dot 97 dot 8 dot 0. Its mask has 24 one bits, so it is slash 24. It is on interface 3.

29
00:03:39,192 --> 00:03:47,683
The next hop is connected. This means that the network is directly attached to B. B delivers the packet to the host directly.

30
00:03:48,133 --> 00:03:50,714
The administrator configures this entry.

31
00:03:51,164 --> 00:04:00,273
Row 3 is the default entry, 0 dot 0 dot 0 dot 0. The next hop is I P 2, on interface 2.

32
00:04:00,723 --> 00:04:11,049
The mask is 0 dot 0 dot 0 dot 0. It has no 1 bits, so the prefix is slash 0. This entry matches every address.

33
00:04:11,499 --> 00:04:14,528
The administrator configures the default entry.

34
00:04:14,978 --> 00:04:27,373
Here are three example packets at router B. These addresses are my examples. They are not on the slide. First, a packet to 73 dot 2 dot 5 dot 9.

35
00:04:27,823 --> 00:04:41,199
Its first 16 bits are 73 dot 2. The entry 73 dot 2 dot 0 dot 0, slash 16, needs exactly these 16 bits. So it matches this entry.

36
00:04:41,649 --> 00:04:45,297
So the packet leaves on interface 1, to I P 1.

37
00:04:45,747 --> 00:04:51,016
Second, a packet to 129 dot 97 dot 8 dot 20.

38
00:04:51,466 --> 00:05:04,970
Its first 24 bits are 129 dot 97 dot 8. So it matches the connected entry, 129 dot 97 dot 8 dot 0, slash 24.

39
00:05:05,420 --> 00:05:09,047
B sends it on interface 3, directly to the host.

40
00:05:09,497 --> 00:05:12,761
Third, a packet to 8 dot 8 dot 8 dot 8.

41
00:05:13,211 --> 00:05:24,176
It does not start with 73 dot 2, and it does not start with 129 dot 97 dot 8. So it matches only the default entry.

42
00:05:24,626 --> 00:05:27,719
So it leaves on interface 2, to I P 2.

43
00:05:28,169 --> 00:05:38,687
So a packet to a connected network goes directly to the host. Every other packet goes to a next-hop router. Next, we look at the exact matching test.

44
00:05:39,137 --> 00:05:46,518
How does the router test if a destination address matches one entry? Slide 53 gives the condition.

45
00:05:46,968 --> 00:05:57,421
Take the entry address from the routing table, x dot y dot z dot w. Here, the entry is 73 dot 2 dot 0 dot 0.

46
00:05:57,871 --> 00:06:08,901
Take the mask of the entry, a dot b dot c dot d. Here, the mask is 255 dot 255 dot 0 dot 0.

47
00:06:09,351 --> 00:06:21,489
The mask has n one bits. Here, 8 plus 8 is 16 one bits, so n is 16. The entry is 73 dot 2 dot 0 dot 0, slash 16.

48
00:06:21,939 --> 00:06:31,198
Take the destination address from the packet. Call it I P 1. Here, I P 1 is 73 dot 2 dot 5 dot 9.

49
00:06:31,648 --> 00:06:34,229
Use the same mask for I P 1.

50
00:06:34,679 --> 00:06:41,250
Do a bitwise AND with the mask on both sides. A mask byte of 255 keeps the byte.

51
00:06:41,700 --> 00:06:44,473
A mask byte of 0 makes the byte 0.

52
00:06:44,923 --> 00:06:48,977
The entry gives 73 dot 2 dot 0 dot 0.

53
00:06:49,427 --> 00:06:54,312
I P 1 also gives 73 dot 2 dot 0 dot 0.

54
00:06:54,762 --> 00:07:00,671
The two results are equal. So the matching is successful. The packet can use this entry.

55
00:07:01,121 --> 00:07:11,681
Now try I P 1 equal to 73 dot 3 dot 5 dot 9. The AND with the mask gives 73 dot 3 dot 0 dot 0.

56
00:07:12,131 --> 00:07:21,454
73 dot 3 dot 0 dot 0 is not equal to 73 dot 2 dot 0 dot 0. So there is no match.

57
00:07:21,904 --> 00:07:35,173
In words: the test asks if I P 1 is in the pool of addresses that x dot y dot z dot w slash n denotes. It compares the n most significant bits of both addresses.

58
00:07:35,623 --> 00:07:46,695
Question bank question 65: why does the routing table store the prefix length as a mask, in the format of an IP address? Pause the video and try it.

59
00:07:47,145 --> 00:07:50,921
A slash 24 prefix becomes 24 one bits.

60
00:07:51,371 --> 00:07:53,313
Then 8 zero bits follow.

61
00:07:53,763 --> 00:08:09,400
In dotted form, that is 255 dot 255 dot 255 dot 0. Eight one bits are 128 plus 64 and so on, plus 1, which is 255.

62
00:08:09,850 --> 00:08:19,834
To test an address, the router does one AND with the mask. Here, I P 1 is 129 dot 97 dot 8 dot 20.

63
00:08:20,284 --> 00:08:23,825
The AND keeps the first 24 bits of I P 1.

64
00:08:24,275 --> 00:08:26,473
The AND clears the last 8 bits.

65
00:08:26,923 --> 00:08:34,432
The result is 129 dot 97 dot 8 dot 0, the network part of I P 1.

66
00:08:34,882 --> 00:08:43,415
The mask is one block of ones, then one block of zeros. So one AND operation gives the network part of any address.

67
00:08:43,865 --> 00:08:52,527
The slide says that this helps an efficient implementation of the matching condition. This is the answer to question 65.

68
00:08:52,977 --> 00:09:03,003
Slide 54 gives the full matching and forwarding algorithm. The first input is I P 1, the destination address from the packet header.

69
00:09:03,453 --> 00:09:06,568
The second input is R T, the routing table.

70
00:09:07,018 --> 00:09:12,266
First, the router tests I P 1 against every entry of the routing table.

71
00:09:12,716 --> 00:09:18,775
If one or more entries match, the router chooses the matching entry with the longest prefix.

72
00:09:19,225 --> 00:09:27,438
It forwards the packet on the interface of that entry. The longest prefix takes the packet closer to the destination network.

73
00:09:27,888 --> 00:09:36,229
If no entry matches, the router checks for a default entry, 0 dot 0 dot 0 dot 0 slash 0.

74
00:09:36,679 --> 00:09:42,141
If the default entry exists, the router forwards the packet on the default interface.

75
00:09:42,591 --> 00:09:49,481
If there is no match and no default entry, the router cannot forward the packet. It drops the packet.

76
00:09:49,931 --> 00:10:00,427
It sends an error message to the source of the packet. This is an I C M P message. I C M P is the Internet Control Message Protocol.

77
00:10:00,877 --> 00:10:08,771
Now the main rule. Slide 55 gives a routing table in binary. A star is a bit that can be anything.

78
00:10:09,221 --> 00:10:14,234
Entry 0 fixes the first 21 bits. So it is slash 21.

79
00:10:14,684 --> 00:10:19,740
Entry 1 fixes the first 24 bits. So it is slash 24.

80
00:10:20,190 --> 00:10:25,118
Entry 2 also fixes 21 bits. So it is slash 21.

81
00:10:25,568 --> 00:10:29,429
Entry 3 is the default. Its prefix length is 0.

82
00:10:29,879 --> 00:10:35,426
The rule: use the longest prefix in the table that matches the destination address.

83
00:10:35,876 --> 00:10:44,431
The first destination is 200 dot 23 dot 22 dot 161. Pause the video and try it.

84
00:10:44,881 --> 00:10:55,825
Entry 0: compare the first 21 bits. Bits 1 to 16 are 200 dot 23. They are the same in every entry and in the destination.

85
00:10:56,275 --> 00:11:08,499
Bits 17 to 21 are the first 5 bits of the third byte. The third byte of the destination is 22, which is 0 0 0 1 0 1 1 0.

86
00:11:08,949 --> 00:11:18,847
Its first 5 bits are 0 0 0 1 0. Entry 0 has 0 0 0 1 0 too. So entry 0 matches.

87
00:11:19,297 --> 00:11:33,612
Entry 1: compare the first 24 bits. Its third byte is 0 0 0 1 1 0 0 0, which is 24. The destination has 22. So entry 1 does not match.

88
00:11:34,062 --> 00:11:46,457
Entry 2: its third byte must start with 0 0 0 1 1. The destination starts with 0 0 0 1 0. Bit 21 differs. No match.

89
00:11:46,907 --> 00:11:53,392
The default entry matches every address. But its prefix length is 0, the shortest possible.

90
00:11:53,842 --> 00:12:00,605
Only entry 0 and the default match. Entry 0 has the longer prefix, 21 bits.

91
00:12:01,055 --> 00:12:03,807
So the packet goes out on interface 0.

92
00:12:04,257 --> 00:12:14,262
The second destination is 200 dot 23 dot 24 dot 170. Use the same table. Pause the video and try it.

93
00:12:14,712 --> 00:12:21,859
The third byte of the destination is 24, which is 0 0 0 1 1 0 0 0.

94
00:12:22,309 --> 00:12:34,362
Entry 0: its third byte must start with 0 0 0 1 0. The destination has 0 0 0 1 1. Bit 21 differs. No match.

95
00:12:34,812 --> 00:12:45,820
Entry 1: the first 24 bits must be 200 dot 23 dot 24. The destination has exactly these bits. Entry 1 matches.

96
00:12:46,270 --> 00:12:57,769
Entry 2: bits 17 to 21 must be 0 0 0 1 1. They are. So entry 2 also matches. Slide 57 shows this match.

97
00:12:58,219 --> 00:13:01,205
The default also matches, with length 0.

98
00:13:01,655 --> 00:13:09,975
Three entries match. Entry 1 has 24 bits, entry 2 has 21 bits, and the default has 0 bits.

99
00:13:10,425 --> 00:13:17,295
The longest is entry 1, as slide 58 shows. So the packet goes out on interface 1.

100
00:13:17,745 --> 00:13:31,675
Entry 1 is a smaller, more specific network inside the range of entry 2. Entry 1 covers only the third byte 24. Entry 2 covers the third bytes 24 to 31.

101
00:13:32,125 --> 00:13:36,456
The longer prefix takes the packet closer to the destination network.

102
00:13:36,906 --> 00:13:50,367
Practice with the same table. These two addresses are my examples, not from the slide. The first address is 200 dot 23 dot 31 dot 5. Pause the video and try it.

103
00:13:50,817 --> 00:13:56,343
The third byte is 31, which is 0 0 0 1 1 1 1 1.

104
00:13:56,793 --> 00:14:06,201
Entry 0 needs the third byte to start with 0 0 0 1 0. Bit 21 of the address is 1. No match.

105
00:14:06,651 --> 00:14:13,264
Entry 1 needs the third byte equal to 24. The address has 31. No match.

106
00:14:13,714 --> 00:14:23,890
Entry 2 needs the third byte to start with 0 0 0 1 1. 31 starts with 0 0 0 1 1. Match.

107
00:14:24,340 --> 00:14:25,897
The default matches too.

108
00:14:26,347 --> 00:14:32,214
Entry 2, slash 21, is the longest match. So the answer is interface 2.

109
00:14:32,664 --> 00:14:38,872
Entry 2 covers the third bytes 24 to 31, and 31 is in this range.

110
00:14:39,322 --> 00:14:45,871
The second address is 200 dot 23 dot 40 dot 1. Pause the video and try it.

111
00:14:46,321 --> 00:14:51,633
The third byte, 40, is 0 0 1 0 1 0 0 0.

112
00:14:52,083 --> 00:14:58,675
Entry 0: bit 19 of the address is 1, but entry 0 needs 0. No match.

113
00:14:59,125 --> 00:15:03,627
Entry 1: the third byte 40 is not 24. No match.

114
00:15:04,077 --> 00:15:07,789
Entry 2: the same bit 19 differs. No match.

115
00:15:08,239 --> 00:15:10,436
Only the default entry matches.

116
00:15:10,886 --> 00:15:15,153
So the packet goes out on interface 3, the default interface.

117
00:15:15,603 --> 00:15:22,835
Without the default entry, the router would drop the packet and send an I C M P error message to the source.

118
00:15:23,285 --> 00:15:36,746
Now the question bank questions on forwarding. Questions 57 and 58: what is table-driven routing, and what two pieces of information does a router use? Pause the video and try it.

119
00:15:37,196 --> 00:15:44,684
Table-driven routing means that the router finds the next hop and the interface by a lookup in its routing table.

120
00:15:45,134 --> 00:15:50,553
The first piece of information is the destination IP address in the packet.

121
00:15:51,003 --> 00:15:55,099
The second piece of information is the routing table in the router.

122
00:15:55,549 --> 00:16:03,357
Questions 59 and 61: show the template of a routing table, and explain its structure. Pause and try it.

123
00:16:03,807 --> 00:16:14,388
Each row has five fields. The destination address and the mask together give a destination network, x dot y dot z dot w slash n.

124
00:16:14,838 --> 00:16:20,001
The next hop is the next router, or connected for a network attached to the router.

125
00:16:20,451 --> 00:16:22,797
The interface is the output port.

126
00:16:23,247 --> 00:16:25,487
The metric is the cost of the route.

127
00:16:25,937 --> 00:16:31,761
Question 60: explain configured, learned and default entries. Pause and try it.

128
00:16:32,211 --> 00:16:39,017
A configured entry is typed in by the administrator, for example a network connected to the router.

129
00:16:39,467 --> 00:16:42,517
A learned entry comes from routing protocols.

130
00:16:42,967 --> 00:16:51,671
A default entry is 0 dot 0 dot 0 dot 0 slash 0. It is configured, and it matches every address.

131
00:16:52,121 --> 00:16:57,583
Question 62 has three parts on prefix matching. Pause the video and try it.

132
00:16:58,033 --> 00:17:15,782
Part a. An entry x dot y dot z dot w slash n matches I P 1 if the AND with the mask gives the same result for both. That is, the n most significant bits are equal. Then I P 1 is in the pool of addresses of that entry.

133
00:17:16,232 --> 00:17:23,101
Part b. Longest prefix matching: among all entries that match, take the one with the largest n.

134
00:17:23,551 --> 00:17:40,597
Part c. The largest n is the most specific network, here the slash 24 inside the slash 21. It takes the packet closest to the destination network. The router forwards the packet on the interface of that entry, to its next hop.

135
00:17:41,047 --> 00:17:50,071
Questions 63 and 64: what happens if no entry matches, and why is a default entry useful? Pause and try it.

136
00:17:50,521 --> 00:17:59,715
If no entry matches and there is no default entry, the router drops the packet and sends an I C M P error message to the source.

137
00:18:00,165 --> 00:18:17,509
A default entry sends every unknown destination to one next hop, for example the router of the I S P. So the table stays small, and packets to unknown networks are not dropped. These two reasons are my reasoning from slide 54.

138
00:18:17,959 --> 00:18:35,922
To recap. The next hop is a function of the destination address and the routing table. Each row has a destination, a mask, a next hop, an interface and a metric. Entries are learned from routing protocols, or configured: connected networks and the default.

139
00:18:36,372 --> 00:18:51,604
An entry matches if the AND with its mask gives the same result for the entry and the destination. The longest matching prefix wins. With no match and no default, the router drops the packet and sends an I C M P error.

140
00:18:52,054 --> 00:18:56,043
The next video, 16 b, covers address aggregation.
